# Fixed point

A **fixed point** of a transformation is an object the transformation sends
to itself: $f(x)=x$. The discussion gives it a sharper reading for this vault.
In its table of transformations, the row "fixed point / reflection" is
described as *folding a transformation onto its representation*. On that
reading a fixed point is the formal shape that self-reference takes: the
place where applying an operation to a description gives back the thing
described.

## Description

### Two lanes

The word covers two situations that behave differently.

| | Fixed point reached by iteration | Fixed point built by self-application |
|---|---|---|
| How it is found | apply $f$ again and again until nothing changes | apply a description to its own code, once |
| What it needs | a process in time that converges | a system that can name its own members |
| Typical case | a stable state of a [[feedback-loop]] | the [[godel-sentence]] |
| Level crossed | none | from an object to its representation and back |

The first lane is where a dynamic system settles. Nothing in it refers to
anything. The second lane is the one that matters for strange loops, and the
rest of this card is about it.

### One construction, four appearances

The discussion names four fixed points of the second kind. They are the same
construction in different settings: take something that expects a description
as input and hand it a description of that very application.

- **In logic.** The [[diagonal-lemma]]: for every formula $\varphi(x)$ there
  is a sentence $G$ with
  $T\vdash G\leftrightarrow\varphi(\ulcorner G\urcorner)$. The sentence is a
  fixed point of $\varphi$ up to provable equivalence. It is not equal to
  $\varphi(\ulcorner G\urcorner)$, only provably equivalent to it.
- **In the lambda calculus.** The fixed-point combinator $Y$ satisfies
  $Y\,f = f\,(Y\,f)$ for every $f$. It is how a language with no built-in
  [[recursion]] obtains it: a recursive function is the fixed point of its
  own defining equation.
- **In computability.** Kleene's recursion theorem: for every total computable
  $f$ there is a program $e$ such that $e$ and $f(e)$ compute the same
  function. Any computable transformation of programs leaves the behaviour of
  some program unchanged.
- **In programs.** A [[quine]] is a program whose output is its own source.
  It is the recursion theorem applied to the transformation "print this text".

The four are listed together in the taxonomy of self-reference, as class B
(self-reproduction) and class C (formal diagonalization). What the fixed
point contributes to each is existence without a search: the self-referring
object is constructed outright, not found by trial.

### What a fixed point does not give

A fixed point is the *existence* of self-reference. It is not yet a
consequence of it. The Gödel sentence exists for every theory that can encode
its syntax, but it yields incompleteness only under hypotheses on the theory.
A quine exists in every reasonable programming language and implies nothing
about the limits of that language. The discussion makes the same point about
quines and reflective interpreters: neither inherently implies incompleteness.

In the discussion's three-layer organisation, fixed points sit in the middle
layer, structural relationships, beside duality, invariance and equivalence.
The reflective phenomena of the top layer are what some fixed points lead to.

### Fixed points of a document

The model of an executable vault is written in the discussion as

$$H_{n+1}=\operatorname{Integrate}(H_n,\operatorname{Eval}(H_n)).$$

A vault $H$ with $\operatorname{Integrate}(H,\operatorname{Eval}(H))=H$ is a
fixed point of the first lane: evaluating it and integrating the result
changes nothing. That is stability, not self-reference. A second-lane fixed
point would be a card whose evaluated content is a statement about that card's
own evaluation. The observer card experiment is an attempt to build the
second kind and to tell it apart from the first.

The identity $L^L=L$ of the constructible universe has the same form: the
operation "build the constructible universe inside" leaves $L$ unchanged. It
is reached by absoluteness and not by diagonalization.

## Relations

- Its logical form is the [[diagonal-lemma]] {relation: example-of}.
- Its best known instance is the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of}, a fixed point of unprovability.
- Its programming instance is the [[quine]] {relation: example-of}, a fixed point of printing.
- It is how [[recursion]] {relation: uses} is obtained in a language without it, through the [[y-combinator]] {relation: example-of}.
- Its computability form is the [[kleene-recursion-theorem]] {relation: example-of}.
- It is built by the inward form of [[diagonalization]] {relation: uses}.
- It is the formal shape of [[self-reference]] {relation: is-a}.
- It differs from the resting state of a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}, which is reached in time and refers to nothing.
- The identity at the centre of the [[constructible-universe]] {relation: analogous-to} has the same form and a different mechanism.
- The [[observer-card-experiment]] {relation: uses} looks for one in a vault.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: a stable vault and a self-describing card are different targets.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[diagonal-lemma]].** Every property of codes has a sentence that
  provably says "my code has this property". It is the fixed-point theorem of
  formal logic.
- **[[quine]].** A program that prints its own source. Seen from here it is a
  fixed point one can run.
- **[[diagonalization]].** Going down the diagonal of a table. Its
  self-applying form is the standard way to construct a fixed point of the
  second lane.
- **[[self-reference]].** A description that includes its own subject. Fixed
  points are how self-reference is made exact without circular definition.

